===Glyph origin===
{{Han etym}}
[[File:京師畯尊-西周早期.jpg|thumb|Jingshijun Zun 京師畯尊, Early Western Zhou period]]
Earliest attested in early Western Zhou bronze inscriptions; the 京師畯尊 preserves the form ⿰氵堇/⿰氵⿱𦰩火. {{Han compound|水|堇|ls=psc|c1=s|c2=p|t1=water}} — name of the {{w|Han River (Shaanxi and Hubei)|Han River}}. {{l|zh|𦰩}} (phonetic; a bound person crying out, probable original form of {{l|zh|嘆}}) became confused with {{l|zh|黃}} as early as the 伯△鼎 (銘續 0213), and by the Spring and Autumn period the corruption was nearly complete. ⿱𦰩火 (transcribable as {{l|zh|堇}} or {{l|zh|熯}}) may relate to the oracle bone sacrificial practice of burning disabled people ({{l|zh|尪}}) to pray for rain (⿱黃火).

The usage for the Chinese ethnicity first appeared during the Northern and Southern Dynasties, claiming the Han dynasty as one of the golden ages of Chinese history.{{rfv-etym|en}} Before the Han dynasty, the Chinese referred their land as [[華夏]] ("Huaxia"), and themselves as [[諸華]] ("various Hua people") or [[諸夏]] ("various people/descendants of the Xias"). "Xia" ("Summer; great, splendid") also gave the name to the first semi-legendary Chinese dynasty, the Xia Dinasty ([[夏朝]]).

